<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #eaf2f8;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> This Beautiful Day</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's Sunny. I'm Happy!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Recognize familiar sounds from audio sources. Interactive: Engage in a role-play based on a listened scenario.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize weather descriptions from audio sources (e.g., "It's sunny."). Can engage in a role-play to show the meaning of simple information about the weather in everyday life (e.g., "What is that?" "An umbrella!" "Why do you need an umbrella?" "It is raining!"). Can get the gist of a simple role-play performed by peers (e.g., after watching a short role-play, they can answer, "Why is Mario sad? "It's raining!").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #eaf2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #eaf2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #eaf2f8; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, everyone!" and displays large flashcards or drawings of different weather conditions (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) and corresponding emotions (happy, sad, bored, excited). The teacher plays short, distinct audio clips of weather sounds (e.g., birds chirping for sunny, rain falling for rainy, wind blowing for windy). Students listen to each sound and point to the corresponding weather flashcard. The teacher then asks, "How do you feel today?" and models "I feel happy!" while pointing to the sunny flashcard and smiling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary and contractions. The teacher holds up a flashcard of a bright sun and says clearly, "It's sunny. I'm happy!" The teacher then holds up an umbrella and says, "It's raining. I'm sad." The teacher emphasizes the /s/ sound in "sunny" and the /r/ sound in "raining," and the contraction "I'm." The teacher uses TPR by making appropriate facial expressions and gestures for each emotion and weather condition. The teacher asks students to repeat chorally after each new word or phrase, focusing on phonemic awareness by blending /s-ʌn-i/ for "sunny" and /r-eɪn/ for "rain."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks for understanding of the new vocabulary and concepts.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a rainy picture) "Is it sunny?" (Expected answer: "No, it's raining.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a happy face) "Am I sad?" (Expected answer: "No, you're happy!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(After playing the sound of wind) "What is that?" (Expected answer: "Wind!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "This Beautiful Day."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue between two children (e.g., Leo and Mia) about the weather and their feelings. The dialogue will use the target vocabulary (sunny, rainy, happy, sad) and grammar (It's, I'm, How do you feel?). Example dialogue: "Hi, Leo! How do you feel today?" "Hi, Mia! I'm happy! It's sunny outside. What about you?" "Oh, I'm happy too. I love sunny days!" "Me too! Let's play!" "Yes!" The audio also includes a second short dialogue: "Oh no! Look, Mia, it's raining!" "Oh no! I'm sad. I can't play outside." "I feel sad too. We need an umbrella." The audio features clear pronunciation, intonation, and pace appropriate for A1.1 learners.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening to and understanding simple descriptions of weather and feelings, and simple questions about them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Before the first listening, the teacher asks, "What is the weather in the first part?" and "How do Leo and Mia feel?" Students listen once to get the main idea.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher provides simple statements related to the dialogue (e.g., "Leo is sad." "It's sunny in the first part.") and students indicate True or False after a second listening.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> The teacher gives students a handout with two columns: one with weather pictures (sun, rain) and the other with feeling emojis (happy, sad). Students listen to the audio again and draw a line to match the weather with the correct feeling for Leo and Mia in both parts of the dialogue.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Recognizing weather descriptions ("It's sunny," "It's raining," "It's cloudy," "It's windy") and expressions of feelings ("I'm happy," "I'm sad," "I'm bored," "I'm excited") from audio. Practice using contractions ('I'm', 'it's') and WH-questions ('How do you feel?').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice: Listen and Point/Circle:</b> The teacher plays audio recordings of single sentences (e.g., "It's sunny." "I'm happy." "It's raining." "I'm sad."). Students have worksheets with three pictures for each sentence (e.g., sun, rain, cloud for "It's sunny"). They listen and point to or circle the correct picture. The teacher repeats the audio as needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled substitution drill: Listen and Complete:</b> The teacher plays an audio track where a sentence is incomplete, and students listen and choose the correct missing word from two spoken options. For example, audio: "It's ______. (sunny/cloudy) I'm happy." Students listen and verbally complete the sentence. Another example: "How do you feel ______? (today/tomorrow)" Students listen and repeat the full question. This reinforces blending spoken phonemes for familiar words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair interaction / Peer check (Listening Game):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of weather and emotion flashcards. Student A holds up a weather flashcard (e.g., "sunny") and Student B listens. Student A then asks, "How do you feel when it's sunny?" Student B listens and responds based on a feeling flashcard they choose, saying, "I'm happy!" They swap roles. The focus is on active listening to the question and then providing a relevant response using the target vocabulary.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "This Beautiful Day."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Role-play in pairs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives each pair a "scenario card" with a weather condition (e.g., "It's sunny," "It's raining," "It's windy") and a corresponding feeling (e.g., "I'm happy," "I'm sad," "I'm excited").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns initiating a short dialogue, asking, "How do you feel today?" Their partner listens carefully and responds based on their scenario card, for example: "I'm happy! It's sunny!" or "I'm sad. It's raining."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They can use simple props like a small toy umbrella, a drawing of a sun, or mimic a coat to show 'cold.'</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The listening component is crucial as students must listen to their partner's question and accurately interpret their scenario card to give an appropriate response. When pairs perform for the class, other students listen to get the gist and identify the weather and feeling.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: Student 1: "How do you feel today?" Student 2 (looking at "It's raining, I'm sad" card): "I'm sad. It's raining!"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play and a simple peer assessment during small group presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their short role-play in front of another pair or the class. The listening component for observers is key: after watching a short role-play, observer students will answer a gist question such as, "Why is Mario sad?" (Expected: "It's raining!") or "How does Lily feel?" (Expected: "She feels happy!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes familiar sounds from audio sources (e.g., weather sounds).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., /s/ in "sunny," /r/ in "raining").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., sun, rain, happy, sad) and grammar (e.g., "I'm," "It's," "How do you feel?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engages in role-play to show meaning of simple information about weather and feelings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Listens actively to partner's question and responds appropriately.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students draw a picture of their favorite weather and write, "It's _______. I'm _______." Then, they complete these sentences:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Today I learned to say: "It's _______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can hear the sound of _______ (e.g., rain/wind).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was your favorite weather sound we listened to today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new word about feelings did you learn?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The weather sound recognition activity in the warm-up worked very well to activate prior knowledge and engage students. The audio dialogue comprehension tasks were effective, especially the matching activity. Some students struggled with distinguishing between "cloudy" and "windy" sounds, which will require more phonemic awareness practice. The pair role-play for production allowed for good listening practice and encouraged simple interactions. For next time, I need to provide more visual cues for different feeling words beyond happy/sad and ensure clearer modeling of the /s/ and /r/ sounds. I will also incorporate more TPR for feeling vocabulary. The overall listening focus was successfully maintained.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will read short, illustrated texts and dialogues that reinforce the same weather and feeling vocabulary ("It's sunny. I'm happy.") from today's listening activities, building on their established recognition of these words.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>